<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul the Apostle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 5 – c. 64/65 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Saul of Tarsus, Saint Paul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul, born Saul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, was a Christian apostle who spread the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in the first century CE and founded several Christian communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the mid-40s to the mid-50s. His life is known chiefly from his letters and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a>. According to Acts, he was a Pharisee who persecuted the followers of Jesus until a vision of Christ on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> led him to be baptised. Fourteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> books are traditionally attributed to him, of which scholars regard seven as undisputed. His death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> is traditional, dated either after the fire of 64 or a few years later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle.jpg" alt="Paul the Apostle" width="330" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresco_of_Saint_Paul_at_Ephesus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paul's Jewish name was Saul, perhaps after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">King Saul</a>, who like Paul belonged to the Tribe of Benjamin. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name Paulus, meaning small, was not a result of his conversion, as is commonly believed, but a second name for communicating with a Greco-Roman audience. He was probably born between 5 BCE and 5 CE, and Acts indicates that he was a Roman citizen by birth, which scholars largely accept.</p><p>His letters say he persecuted early Christians beyond measure. His conversion has been dated to 31 to 36 CE from his own letters, and Acts says he was healed of blindness and baptised by Ananias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. Three years after his conversion he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, where he met James and stayed 15 days with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-peter">Simon Peter</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A meeting with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> church took place in 49 CE by traditional and majority dating. The question was whether gentile converts needed to be circumcised, and Peter, James and John, whom Paul calls the Pillars of the Church, accepted his mission to the gentiles. Paul later recounted publicly confronting Peter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> over his reluctance to share a meal with gentile Christians.</p><p>Around 50 to 51 CE Paul reached Corinth, where he founded the church and stayed 18 months, a date fixed by the reference in Acts to the proconsul Gallio. He then stayed almost three years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, and left after a silversmith incited riots against his preaching. According to the traditional account he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> around 60 and spent two years under house arrest, where the narrative of Acts ends.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Paul's death is believed to have occurred after the Great Fire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in July 64 and before the last year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s reign in 68; Acts does not mention it. According to the Liber Pontificalis he was buried outside the walls of Rome, on the Via Ostiensis. Seven of the letters that bear his name, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, Galatians and Philippians, are almost universally accepted as his own.</p><p>Paul's influence on Christian thinking has arguably been more significant than that of any other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> author. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> gave his life to Christ after reading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> 13, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s reading of Romans shaped his doctrine of sola fide. Some critics have accused him of corrupting the teaching of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, while other scholars see him as a fundamentally Jewish figure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paul the Apostle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Paul of Tarsus</b>, a Jewish convert who had at first persecuted the believers, became the great missionary to the Gentiles.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Thirteen letters present <b>Paul the Apostle</b> as their author: nine to churches and four to individuals.</blockquote><a href="/work/new-testament">New Testament · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
